# DESCRIPTION

The console shows the system log on the screen.
It maps the framebuffer that the firmware gave the boot (written with write combining), reads the log as it is written, and draws each record as a line of text.
A manifest mounts its tree at `/mnt/console`.

Text is a grid of cells.
The font is Spleen, 16 by 32 pixels on a screen 2560 pixels wide or more and 8 by 16 on a smaller one, and the grid is as many cells as fit.
The first line it draws names the screen and the grid.
Each log record is drawn as the kernel's console prints it, `[sssss.mmm] ppppp:ttttt> text`, the time in seconds and milliseconds since the kernel started and the process and thread that logged it.
The line is amber for a warning and magenta for an error, and green otherwise.

The log is a ring of 512 records of 256 bytes, read from the oldest kept.
If the console falls behind, the ring overwrites records it has not read, and `status` counts them as dropped.
kbdfs(4) writes what is typed, and what is written to its `cons`, to `write`, so the console is the terminal of the shell on it, `rc` (rc(1)), and its programs; their text and the log share the screen.
It keeps the last 64 KiB of the log's lines as text, for `kprint`, which manifests mount at `/dev/kprint` (log(1)).

Where radharc(4) runs, it takes the framebuffer from the console through `handoff` (radharc-handoff(5)).
The console stops drawing to the framebuffer and opens a window of Radharc's, as many cells of the font as fit the screen less a margin, up to 100 by 30; it moves the grid's text into the window's grid and draws there from then on, presenting the rows that changed.

A grid takes UTF-8.
It writes a printable character at the cursor, wrapping at the right edge, and scrolls when the cursor leaves the bottom.
A newline, a carriage return and a tab (to the next multiple of eight columns) move the cursor, and a backspace erases the character before it; other control characters and ANSI escape sequences (`ESC [` to its final byte) are dropped.
A byte that is not UTF-8 is drawn as U+FFFD, and a character the font lacks, Spleen having no U+FFFD, as a question mark.

The manifest asks for what the console needs:

```text
service console
program console
resource mmio
bootdata
debuglog
export
early
restart on-failure
```

`resource mmio` and `bootdata` give it the framebuffer, which the boot data names, and `debuglog` the log's ring and its doorbell.
It starts early, to show the boot from its start.
Without a linear framebuffer of 32-bit pixels in the boot data it exits with a message; without the log it runs, and the log is not shown.

# FILES

: `/mnt/console/status`
  Read only.
  The framebuffer (`framebuffer <w>x<h> stride <s> <format>`, the stride in pixels and the format `rgbx888` or `bgr888x`), the font (`font spleen <w>x<h>`), when the first frame was drawn (`first frame <n> ns`, on the monotonic clock, which starts with the kernel), the grid (`grid <columns>x<rows>`), the records shown and dropped (`records <n> dropped <n>`) and the frames drawn and the time spent drawing them (`frames <n> drawing <n> us`), a line each.
  Then, once Radharc has taken the framebuffer, `handed over` until the window is open, and `window <w>x<h> presents <n> frames <n>` after.
: `/mnt/console/screen`
  Read only.
  The grid's text, a line for each row, trailing spaces dropped.
: `/mnt/console/write`
  Text written here is shown at the cursor, in the ink color, a pale blue, and drawn as soon as it is written.
  Reads give nothing.
: `/mnt/console/kprint`
  The log as text, each record's line as the screen shows it: a reader starts at the oldest line kept and, once it has read all there is, waits for the next.
  Each channel opened on it reads on its own; one that falls more than 64 KiB behind skips to the oldest line kept.
: `/mnt/console/handoff`
  A service, radharc-handoff(5): the compositor takes the framebuffer, and the console moves into a window of its.

